---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { GITHUB_URL } from "../config/site";
import { breadcrumbs } from "../data/jsonld";
const desc = "Showsteps stores everything on your device, has no account and no analytics, and makes no network requests after install. Every permission explained.";
---
<Base title="Privacy" description={desc} path="/privacy/" jsonLd={[breadcrumbs([{ name: "Showsteps", path: "/" }, { name: "Privacy", path: "/privacy/" }])]}>
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="page-head">
      <span class="eyebrow">Privacy</span>
      <h1>Nothing leaves your browser</h1>
      <p class="lede">Short version: Showsteps has no server, no account and no analytics. What you record stays on your computer until you export a file yourself.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-wrap">
      <div class="prose">
        <h2 style="margin-top:0">What Showsteps stores, and where</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>Guides, step text and screenshots are stored in your browser's IndexedDB on your computer.</li>
          <li>Settings (for example highlight colour) are stored in the extension's local storage.</li>
          <li>Exports are files you save. Showsteps does not track where you send them.</li>
        </ul>
        <h2>What leaves your computer</h2>
        <p>
          Nothing. The extension makes no network requests after install: no analytics, no crash reporting, no update pings of its own
          (Chrome updates extensions itself), and no AI service. When you press a support link, your browser opens this website in a
          normal tab; that is separate from the extension.
        </p>
        <h2>Passwords and sensitive fields</h2>
        <p>
          While you record, Showsteps looks for password fields, card numbers and other fields that look sensitive. The typed value is
          not saved, and the step screenshot is blurred over the field. Auto-detection is a safety net, not a guarantee: it cannot know
          that a name in a table is a real customer. Review every screenshot before sharing, and use the blur and crop tools.
        </p>
        <h2>Permissions, and why</h2>
        <div class="table-scroll" role="region" aria-label="Permissions" tabindex="0">
          <table class="compare">
            <thead><tr><th scope="col">Permission</th><th scope="col">Why Showsteps needs it</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><th scope="row"><code>activeTab</code></th><td>Lets Showsteps capture and act on the tab you are working in when you press Record.</td></tr>
              <tr><th scope="row"><code>scripting</code></th><td>Injects the small script that notices clicks, typing and selections and describes the element.</td></tr>
              <tr><th scope="row"><code>storage</code></th><td>Keeps your settings.</td></tr>
              <tr><th scope="row"><code>sidePanel</code></th><td>Shows the step list and editor beside the page.</td></tr>
              <tr><th scope="row"><code>unlimitedStorage</code></th><td>Guides with many screenshots are large. This stops the browser from clearing them to save space.</td></tr>
              <tr><th scope="row">Access to all sites (optional)</th><td>Requested only when you start recording, so Showsteps can follow you across tabs and page changes. You can decline it and record a single tab.</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <h2>This website</h2>
        <p>
          The site you are reading has no analytics, no cookies and no third-party requests. Its fonts are served from the same origin.
        </p>
        <h2>Payments</h2>
        <p>
          Support payments are handled by Stripe on Stripe's own pages. Showsteps never sees your card details, and the extension does not
          know whether you have given.
        </p>
        <h2>Changes and questions</h2>
        <p>
          The source is on <a href={GITHUB_URL}>GitHub</a>, so any change to this behaviour is visible in the history. Questions can go
          to the <a href={`${GITHUB_URL}/issues`}>issue tracker</a>.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</Base>
